  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe potential for clusters of showers/thunderstorms today and \ncontinued spread among hi res solutions in their forecast evolution \nof convection during the first half of the day leads to continued \nuncertainty with respect to timing and severe weather potential. \nFrequent meso anaylsis throughout the day will be in order to fully \nguage severe potential through the course of today. \n\nOvernight meso analysis indicates the edge of the elevated mixed \nlayer extending into Lower Michigan, contributing to 0-3km MU CAPE \nof 1500 to 2000 J/kg. Regional radar mosaic shows an axis of \nconvection extending along the edge of the mid level cap and within \nthe gradient of a high instability axis extending from the Arrowhead \nof Minnesota down to northeast Wisconsin. Another cluster of \nthunderstorms over eastern Upper Mi is tied to a mid level short \nwave impulse, forecast to slide across Lake Huron and portions of Se \nMi early this morning. This will at least offer the potential for \nelevated convection to slide into portions of the forecast area from \nthe north early. Veering of the low level inflow across the western \nGreat Lakes this morning will attempt to drive an influx of higher \nsfc based instability into southern Lower Mi. This will be \ncontingent upon the evolution of convection across Wisconsin/NE \nMinnesota and the extent of any elevated morning convection dropping \ninto the area from the north. The potential for the aforementioned \nconvection to the northwest to evolve into a forward propagating MCS \nwith a robust rear inflow jet has resulted in SPC upgrading SW Lower\nMi and the swrn portions of the DTX CWA from a slight to enhanced \nrisk. \n\nStronger larger scale ascent along the nose of a 110 knot upper jet \nand additional mid level short wave energy will arrive across srn Mi \nlate this afternoon and evening. This will support a high \nprobability of showers and thunderstorms. Severe weather potential \nduring this time period will have a large dependance on the coverage \nof early day convection and the efficiency with which it erodes the \ninstability resorvoir if at all. The variation in how hi res \nsolutions handle early day convection also results in a wide array \nof forecast storm relative helicity and mid level shear values. This \nagain highlights the importance frequent meso analysis will have \nduring the course of the day. \n\nMid level trough amplification across the eastern Great Lakes \novernight into Tuesday will drive cooler and less humid air into \nLower Mi, which will persist through mid week. Model soundings \nsuggest the depth of diurnal cu development on Tuesday may support a \nfew light showers/sprinkes. Mid level anticyclonic flow and sfc high \npressure will envelop the region by Wednesday, warranting a dry \nforecast.",
